        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: What is Thomas Rich's military rank?\n\nAnswer A: Admiral\n  about: Thomas Rich\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 1801\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Rich was promoted to Rear-Admiral of the Blue in 1794, and later to Vice-Admiral of the Red in 1799. The most significant evidence of Rich's admiral rank comes from his appointment as Admiral of the Blue in 1801.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Chief of Artillery\n  about: Thomas Rich\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"He held the rank of Chief of Artillery.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: **Thomas Rich**\n\nThomas Rich (1733-1803) was a British politician and naval officer who served as a Member of Parliament (MP) and held the senior rank of Admiral in the Royal Navy of the United Kingdom. **Early Life and Career**\n\nThomas Rich was born in 1733 to a prominent family in England. Little is known about his early life, but he began his naval career at a young age, rising through the ranks of the Royal Navy. **Naval Career**\n\nRich's naval career spanned several decades, during which he served with distinction and earned promotions to increasingly senior ranks. According to the Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, Rich was promoted to Rear-Admiral of the Blue in 1794, and later to Vice-Admiral of the Red in 1799. [1] This progression of ranks demonstrates his growing expertise and leadership abilities within the Royal Navy. **Admiral Rank**\n\nThe most significant evidence of Rich's admiral rank comes from his appointment as Admiral of the Blue in 1801. [2] This senior rank is one of the highest in the Royal Navy, and Rich's achievement of it is a testament to his dedication and service to the British naval forces. **Parliamentary Career**\n\nIn addition to his naval career, Rich also served as a Member of Parliament (MP) for the constituency of Sandwich from 1784 to 1790. [3] This dual role demonstrates his commitment to public service and his ability to balance the demands of both military and political careers. **References**\n\n[1] Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, \"Rich, Thomas (1733-1803)\" (Oxford University Press, 2004)\n\n[2] The London Gazette, \"No. 15415\" (February 14, 1801)\n\n[3] History of Parliament Online, \"RICH, Thomas (1733-1803)\" (Institute of Historical Research, 2019)\n\nThis Wikipedia entry presents comprehensive evidence for the statement that Thomas Rich holds the military rank of Admiral.\nDocument 2: Thomas Rich\n\nThomas Rich (1733-1803) was a British Member of Parliament and a distinguished military officer who held the rank of Chief of Artillery. Early Life and Education\n\nThomas Rich was born on February 12, 1733, in London, England, to a family of modest means. He received his early education at Eton College, where he demonstrated a keen interest in mathematics and science. Rich's academic prowess earned him a scholarship to Christ Church, Oxford, where he studied military tactics and strategy. Military Career\n\nRich's military career began in 1755, when he enlisted as a lieutenant in the British Army's Royal Artillery Regiment. He saw action in the Seven Years' War, participating in the Battle of Quebec in 1759. Rich's bravery and tactical acumen during the battle caught the attention of his superiors, who promoted him to captain in 1762. In 1775, Rich was appointed as the Chief of Artillery for the British Expeditionary Force during the American Revolutionary War. He played a crucial role in the Battle of Bunker Hill, where his artillery tactics helped to repel the American forces. Rich's leadership and expertise earned him the respect of his peers and the admiration of his superiors. Parliamentary Career\n\nIn 1784, Rich retired from active military service and entered politics. He was elected as a Member of Parliament for the constituency of Windsor, a seat he held until his death in 1803. During his parliamentary career, Rich was a vocal advocate for military reform and modernization. He chaired several committees on military affairs and was instrumental in shaping British military policy during the late 18th century. Studies and References\n\n* \"The Artillery Tactics of Thomas Rich\" by Major General John Smith, Journal of Military History, Vol. 12, No. 3 (1995)\n* \"Thomas Rich: A Biographical Study\" by Dr. Emily Johnson, Oxford University Press (2001)\n* \"The British Army in the American Revolutionary War\" by Dr. James Wilson, Routledge (2010)\n* \"The Parliamentary Career of Thomas Rich\" by Dr. Robert Brown, Journal of Parliamentary History, Vol. 20, No. 2 (2015)\n\nNotes\n\n^ \"The Artillery Tactics of Thomas Rich\" by Major General John Smith, Journal of Military History, Vol. 12, No.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
